{"subjectId":"deep-learning","id":"pytorch-training-loop","title":"The Training Loop","group":"PyTorch in Practice","order":15,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Every PyTorch project has the same heartbeat: for each batch — forward, loss, zero_grad, backward, step. Around it: an evaluation loop in <code>eval()</code> mode under <code>no_grad()</code>, and checkpoints.</p>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>model.train()</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\">Dropout on, BatchNorm uses batch statistics.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Forward</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>logits = model(xb)</code> then <code>loss = loss_fn(logits, yb)</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>optimizer.zero_grad()</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\">Clear gradients from the previous step.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>loss.backward()</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\">Compute gradients.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">5</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>optimizer.step()</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\">Update weights (then <code>scheduler.step()</code> if you use one).</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">6</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Evaluate</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>model.eval()</code> + <code>torch.no_grad()</code> on the validation set; keep the best checkpoint.</div></div></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-player-play\"></i>A complete example</h3>\n<p>Classify points by whether they lie inside a circle — a non-linear boundary a linear model can't learn.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import torch\nfrom torch import nn\nfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader, TensorDataset\n\ntorch.manual_seed(0)\nX = torch.rand(1000, 2) * 2 - 1                      # points in [-1, 1]²\ny = ((X ** 2).sum(dim=1) &lt; 0.5).long()               # class 1 inside the circle\ntrain_ds, val_ds = TensorDataset(X[:800], y[:800]), TensorDataset(X[800:], y[800:])\ntrain_loader = DataLoader(train_ds, batch_size=32, shuffle=True)\nval_loader = DataLoader(val_ds, batch_size=200)\n\nmodel = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(2, 32), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(32, 32), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(32, 2))\nloss_fn = nn.CrossEntropyLoss()\noptimizer = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-2)\n\ndef evaluate(loader):\n    model.eval()\n    correct = total = 0\n    with torch.no_grad():\n        for xb, yb in loader:\n            correct += (model(xb).argmax(dim=1) == yb).sum().item()\n            total += len(yb)\n    return correct / total\n\nfor epoch in range(20):\n    model.train()\n    for xb, yb in train_loader:\n        loss = loss_fn(model(xb), yb)\n        optimizer.zero_grad()\n        loss.backward()\n        optimizer.step()\n\nprint(f\"train acc {evaluate(train_loader):.2f}, val acc {evaluate(val_loader):.2f}\")   # train acc 0.99, val acc 0.97</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-device-floppy\"></i>Saving and loading</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import torch\nfrom torch import nn\n\ntorch.manual_seed(0)\nmodel = nn.Linear(3, 1)\noptimizer = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-3)\n\ntorch.save(model.state_dict(), \"model.pt\")                   # weights only — the usual way\ntorch.save({\"epoch\": 5, \"model\": model.state_dict(),          # full checkpoint to resume training\n            \"optimizer\": optimizer.state_dict()}, \"checkpoint.pt\")\n\nrestored = nn.Linear(3, 1)                                    # rebuild the architecture first…\nrestored.load_state_dict(torch.load(\"model.pt\"))              # …then load the weights\nrestored.eval()\nx = torch.ones(1, 3)\nprint(torch.equal(model(x), restored(x)))                     # True\n\nckpt = torch.load(\"checkpoint.pt\")\nprint(ckpt[\"epoch\"], list(ckpt[\"model\"].keys()))              # 5 ['weight', 'bias']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>A <code>state_dict</code> is an ordered dict of parameter and buffer tensors keyed by name. Save that (not the whole pickled model object) — it survives code refactors and is safe to load with <code>torch.load(..., weights_only=True)</code>, the default in recent PyTorch.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cpu\"></i>Using a GPU</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>device = torch.device(\"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available()\n                      else \"mps\" if torch.backends.mps.is_available() else \"cpu\")\nmodel = model.to(device)                     # parameters move once\nfor xb, yb in train_loader:\n    xb, yb = xb.to(device), yb.to(device)    # every batch must follow\n    ...\n# load a GPU checkpoint on a CPU-only machine:\nstate = torch.load(\"model.pt\", map_location=\"cpu\")</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Call <code>model.eval()</code> and wrap validation/inference in <code>torch.no_grad()</code> (or <code>torch.inference_mode()</code>); log <code>loss.item()</code>, not the tensor.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Accumulate <code>total_loss += loss</code> — it keeps every step's graph alive and leaks memory.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Walk me through a PyTorch training step.</summary><div><p>Put the model in train mode, move the batch to the device, run the forward pass to get logits, compute the loss, call <code>optimizer.zero_grad()</code>, <code>loss.backward()</code> to compute gradients, optionally clip them, then <code>optimizer.step()</code> to update the weights (and step the LR scheduler).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does model.eval() change?</summary><div><p>It switches layers that behave differently at inference: dropout is disabled and BatchNorm uses its running statistics. It does <strong>not</strong> disable gradient tracking — that's what <code>torch.no_grad()</code> is for.</p></div></details></section>"}